<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu'l-Hayja Wahsudan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1019–1059</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abu'l-Hayja Wahsudan was the Rawwadid emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, ruling north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> from about 1019 to 1059. He fought the Armenian kingdoms and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a> and welcomed the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qatran-tabrizi">Qatran Tabrizi</a>, whose verses record the destructive earthquake of 1042 at Tabriz. In 1054 he submitted to the Seljuk sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughril">Tughril</a>.</p></div>
